I have analyzed the snapshot for Week 5, Lesson 1.

This quiz shifts gears from pure G-code math to the broader CAD/CAM workflow and CNC machine architecture. It’s a great conceptual check!

Here is the breakdown of the new questions:

Q1 (CAD/CAM Workflow): The standard process flows from Shape Modeling (D) → Defining the Machining Process (A) → Generating Cutter Location Data (B) → Post-Processing into G-Code (E) → Finally Machining the part (C).

Q2 (Hardware): The MCU, DPU, and CLU are all physical hardware components of the machine's controller. The Part Program is purely software (data/instructions).

Q3 (Part Program Data): G-code/Part programs handle direct machine actions (tool changes, coolant, movement paths). They do not store high-level design metadata like the "types of curves" (e.g., splines or NURBS) used during the CAD phase.

Q4 (2-Axis Limitations): A 2-axis machine can easily perform linear moves, positioning, and circular interpolation (drawing circles on a flat plane). However, Helical Interpolation (like cutting a screw thread) requires simultaneous movement in X, Y, and Z, making it impossible for a 2-axis system.

